      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nps.gov/places/000/lobo-canyon.htm",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A hard calcite crust forms on the canyon’s surface, later eroding to reveal wind‑sculpted tafoni.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nps.gov/places/000/lobo-canyon.htm",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 27‑acre block slide, likely from the 1812 Santa Barbara Channel earthquake, sits just before the road climbs west out of the canyon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nps.gov/places/000/lobo-canyon.htm",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The slide has shifted roughly 100 feet northeast, creating a natural dam that holds ponded sediment upstream.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nps.gov/places/000/lobo-canyon.htm",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Downstream of the slide, a dense stand of oaks, toyons, and a perennial spring thrive, fed by the blocked water flow.",
